t stimuli","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":748.1946818033854,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":416.6666666666667,"r":550.6666666666666,"t":757.5280151367188},"charspan":[0,45],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/1","text":"object-based attention for irrelevant stimuli"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"The same participant with high miss-rates also had the poorest accuracy (87%). After excluding this participant; the filtered accuracy data were analyzed in repeated-measures ANOVA, with attended hemifield (Left vs. Right) and Grouping (Grouped VS. Ungrouped stimuli) as repeated-measures factors _ There was significant main effect only for Attention; with higher accuracy for discriminating check-size changes in the left hemifield = 9.75, p = 0.02]. Mean (and SD) for left was 94% (0.3%) and for right; 91% (1%) . There was no significant interaction with grouping. A similar pattern was observed with the full data set.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":603.1946818033854,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":43.666666666666664,"r":294.0,"t":719.1946818033854},"charspan":[0,623],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/2","text":"The same participant with high miss-rates also had the poorest accuracy (87%). After excluding this participant; the filtered accuracy data were analyzed in repeated-measures ANOVA, with attended hemifield (Left vs. Right) and Grouping (Grouped VS. Ungrouped stimuli) as repeated-measures factors _ There was significant main effect only for Attention; with higher accuracy for discriminating check-size changes in the left hemifield = 9.75, p = 0.02]. Mean (and SD) for left was 94% (0.3%) and for right; 91% (1%) . There was no significant interaction with grouping. A similar pattern was observed with the full data set."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"section_header","level":1,"orig":"WHOLE BRAIN ANALYSES","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":584.8613484700521,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":44.666666666666664,"r":137.33333333333334,"t":594.8613484700521},"charspan":[0,20],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/3","text":"WHOLE BRAIN ANALYSES"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Statistical contrasts of left vs. right cued attention revealed signif icant activations (family-wise corrected for multiple comparisons at p 0.05) contralateral to the cued hemifield in cuneus and lingual gyrus (see Table 1 for coordinates). Lowered thresholds (p < 0.001 uncorrected, see Figure 3, left) revealed widespread areas contralateral to the cued hemifield. Event-related contrasts of left vs. right transients showed significant activations contralateral to the transient (corrected for multiple comparisons at p < 0.05) in lingual  gyrus and fusiform gyrus p 0.001 were seen in occipital inferior and superior occipital areas putatively within area V3a (Tootell et al., 1995). Contrasts of grouped VS. ungrouped stim uli revealed no notable activations even at lowered thresholds (p 0.001 uncorrected), for either event-related or block-related analyses.  There were also no significant results for whole-brain block and event-related analyses of specific interactions between grouping and attention. (e.g","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":375.86134847005206,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":42.666666666666664,"r":294.0,"t":584.5280151367188},"charspan":[0,1018],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/4","text":"Statistical contrasts of left vs. right cued attention revealed signif icant activations (family-wise corrected for multiple comparisons at p 0.05) contralateral to the cued hemifield in cuneus and lingual gyrus (see Table 1 for coordinates). Lowered thresholds (p < 0.001 uncorrected, see Figure 3, left) revealed widespread areas contralateral to the cued hemifield. Event-related contrasts of left vs. right transients showed significant activations contralateral to the transient (corrected for multiple comparisons at p < 0.05) in lingual  gyrus and fusiform gyrus p 0.001 were seen in occipital inferior and superior occipital areas putatively within area V3a (Tootell et al., 1995). Contrasts of grouped VS. ungrouped stim uli revealed no notable activations even at lowered thresholds (p 0.001 uncorrected), for either event-related or block-related analyses.  There were also no significant results for whole-brain block and event-related analyses of specific interactions between grouping and attention. (e.g"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"section_header","level":1,"orig":"RETINOTOPY: BLOCKED ANALYSES","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":357.52801513671875,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":44.666666666666664,"r":168.0,"t":367.52801513671875},"charspan":[0,28],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/5","text":"RETINOTOPY: BLOCKED ANALYSES"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Beta weights (representing the overall level of BOLD activation) for each of the block-related conditions were estimated from each of the ROIs for each participant (i.e. masked retinotopic visual areas including hMTIVS+, see Methods) . As we had no specific hypotheses about the laterality of attentional effects, data from left and right hemispheres were pooled according to whether the respective contralateral visual hemifield was attended or ignored. Data were entered into 3-way repeated measures ANOVA with the following factors: Attention whether given ROI was contralateral vs. ipsilateral to the attended hemifield) , Grouping, and cortical Area.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":231.52801513671875,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":43.0,"r":294.6666666666667,"t":355.86134847005206},"charspan":[0,655],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/6","text":"Beta weights (representing the overall level of BOLD activation) for each of the block-related conditions were estimated from each of the ROIs for each participant (i.e. masked retinotopic visual areas including hMTIVS+, see Methods) . As we had no specific hypotheses about the laterality of attentional effects, data from left and right hemispheres were pooled according to whether the respective contralateral visual hemifield was attended or ignored. Data were entered into 3-way repeated measures ANOVA with the following factors: Attention whether given ROI was contralateral vs. ipsilateral to the attended hemifield) , Grouping, and cortical Area."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"caption","orig":"Table MNI coordinates (mm) of regions identified in whole-brain contrasts, significant at p 0.05 corrected.","parent":{"$ref":"#/tables/0"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":187.52801513671875,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":44.0,"r":284.0,"t":208.86134847005212},"charspan":[0,107],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/7","text":"Table MNI coordinates (mm) of regions identified in whole-brain contrasts, significant at p 0.05 corrected."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"MNI coordinates (mm).","parent":{"$ref":"#/groups/0"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":62.86134847005212,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":48.0,"r":120.66666666666667,"t":70.86134847005212},"charspan":[0,21],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/8","text":"MNI coordinates (mm)."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Initial analysis revealed a main effect of Attention; with larger BOLD signals when attention was cued to the contralateral vs. =70.69, p = 0.0001]; and main effect   of Grouping,   with   greater   signals  for   ungrouped   than = 9.29, p = 0.019]. There was main effect of Area [F(s, 35) =23.86, p 0.0001], which was partially accounted for by higher signal estimates in hMT/VS+ (9.87, SD 0.14) compared to the other areas (mean 4.86, SD 0.87)Variability was also much higher in MT/V5+ (SD0.14) compared to other areas (mean SD 0.022, SD 0.017). The only signifi cant interaction was between Attention and Area [F(s, 35) 4.11, p = 0.005] with larger effects of attention to the contralateral vs. ipsilateral hemifield in hMT/VS+ than in the other visual areas (see Supplementary Figure 1).","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":557.1946818033854,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":301.6666666666667,"r":552.0,"t":719.5280151367188},"charspan":[0,792],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/9","text":"Initial analysis revealed a main effect of Attention; with larger BOLD signals when attention was cued to the contralateral vs. =70.69, p = 0.0001]; and main effect   of Grouping,   with   greater   signals  for   ungrouped   than = 9.29, p = 0.019]. There was main effect of Area [F(s, 35) =23.86, p 0.0001], which was partially accounted for by higher signal estimates in hMT/VS+ (9.87, SD 0.14) compared to the other areas (mean 4.86, SD 0.87)Variability was also much higher in MT/V5+ (SD0.14) compared to other areas (mean SD 0.022, SD 0.017). The only signifi cant interaction was between Attention and Area [F(s, 35) 4.11, p = 0.005] with larger effects of attention to the contralateral vs. ipsilateral hemifield in hMT/VS+ than in the other visual areas (see Supplementary Figure 1)."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"All further  analyses   excluded the participant with poor hit rates, in case this was indicative of poorly controlled attention: To render the variances between all ROI areas more uniform, we normalized each participants beta estimates for each given con dition from each given ROI (pooling data across hemispheres, as described above) by subtracting the average (and dividing by the standard deviation) of block-related beta estimates obtained across all conditions from the same bilateral ROIs. Normalized data now had similar means and ranges across ROIs and par ticipants, while the detailed pattern of results across conditions remained   unchanged   within each ROI. An ANOVA based on the normalized data now revealed significant interaction of Attention and Grouping [F(, 6) =8.06, p = 0.03], with greater attentional modulation in the grouped compared to ungrouped condition (see Figure 4). These analyses also confirmed the main effect of Attention  [F(, 6) 705, p 0.0001] and   Grouping [F(, 6) 22.12, p = 0.003], and no main effect or interaction for Area.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":354.19468180338544,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":301.3333333333333,"r":552.3333333333334,"t":558.5280151367188},"charspan":[0,1068],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/10","text":"All further  analyses   excluded the participant with poor hit rates, in case this was indicative of poorly controlled attention: To render the variances between all ROI areas more uniform, we normalized each participants beta estimates for each given con dition from each given ROI (pooling data across hemispheres, as described above) by subtracting the average (and dividing by the standard deviation) of block-related beta estimates obtained across all conditions from the same bilateral ROIs. Normalized data now had similar means and ranges across ROIs and par ticipants, while the detailed pattern of results across conditions remained   unchanged   within each ROI. An ANOVA based on the normalized data now revealed significant interaction of Attention and Grouping [F(, 6) =8.06, p = 0.03], with greater attentional modulation in the grouped compared to ungrouped condition (see Figure 4). These analyses also confirmed the main effect of Attention  [F(, 6) 705, p 0.0001] and   Grouping [F(, 6) 22.12, p = 0.003], and no main effect or interaction for Area."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Separate analyses were conducted for each visual area showed that the interaction between grouping and attention was sig nificant in V2 6) =13.62, p = 0.01], and borderline sig nificant in VI =5.40, p = 0.059] (see   Figure 5) . A","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":305.19468180338544,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":302.0,"r":551.6666666666666,"t":352.86134847005206},"charspan":[0,230],"page